# Miniforge Rust shape

Project-specific architectural constraints for Rust code in Miniforge product-family repos
(miniforge, miniforge-control, thesium-* products, fleet, etc.).

Sits on top of the language rules:

- `languages/rust` (230) — base style + idioms.
- `languages/rust-async` (231), `rust-unsafe` (232), `rust-observability` (233), `rust-wire-protocols` (234).

The frame: Clippy catches idiom violations; these rules catch **semantic Rust design drift** that's
specific to a governed workflow-orchestration runtime. They're the layer between linting and
architecture review.

## Workflow execution state is typed, never stringly

No `"running"` / `"failed"` / `"done"` state transitions in core logic. Workflow phases, agent
sessions, policy evaluations, PR train slots — every closed state machine is an enum with data on
the variants. See `languages/rust` (230) "Domain newtypes at boundaries" for the general rule;
this is the Miniforge-specific application.

```rust
// Good
enum PhaseState {
    Pending,
    Running { started_at: OffsetDateTime },
    Succeeded { evidence: EvidenceBundleId },
    Failed { error: PhaseError },
    Cancelled { reason: CancellationReason },
}

// Bad — stringly state with implicit cross-field invariants
struct Phase {
    state: String,                 // "pending" | "running" | "done" | "failed" | ...
    error: Option<String>,         // populated only if state == "failed"
    evidence_id: Option<String>,   // populated only if state == "done"
}
```

The stringly form encodes invariants ("error present iff failed", "evidence present iff done") as
runtime contracts you have to remember in every code path. New states get added by typoing a new
string. Exhaustive matching on the enum form gives you compiler pressure at every existing call
site.

## Policy decisions are values

A policy decision is a structured value carrying its own justification or rejection reason. Not
`bool`. Not `(bool, String)`. Not `Option<String>`. Not `Result<Allow, Deny>` (because review
isn't an error).

```rust
pub enum PolicyDecision {
    Allow {
        evidence: Vec<EvidenceRef>,
    },
    Deny {
        violations: Vec<PolicyViolation>,
    },
    RequiresReview {
        questions: Vec<ReviewQuestion>,
    },
}
```

Three first-class outcomes — `Allow`, `Deny`, `RequiresReview` — with the data each needs.
Downstream consumers match exhaustively; adding a fourth outcome (e.g. `Defer`) lights up every
call site at compile time.

Operator visibility: every decision serializes losslessly to evidence; the audit trail can replay
exactly which decision was made and why.

## Mechanical detection returns structured findings

Linters, policy checks, drift detectors, attention derivers — anything that produces a "finding"
about an artifact — emits a structured `Finding`, not a string.

```rust
pub struct Finding {
    pub rule_id: RuleId,
    pub severity: Severity,
    pub span: Option<SourceSpan>,
    pub message: DiagnosticMessage,
    pub evidence: Vec<EvidenceRef>,
}
```

Renderers downstream (TUI, web dashboard, FFI to native app, exported reports) format the finding
per the consumer's needs. The producer's contract is the struct shape, not the rendered text.

`DiagnosticMessage` is itself localized via the project's `mfc-strings` framework — never a raw
English literal. See `foundations/localization` (050).

## Adapters behind traits; domain policy concrete

Use traits for I/O boundaries:

```rust
pub trait GitProvider: Send + Sync {
    async fn fetch_pr(&self, repo: RepoId, pr: PrNumber) -> Result<Pr>;
    async fn open_pr(&self, draft: PrDraft) -> Result<Pr>;
}

pub trait LlmClient: Send + Sync { /* ... */ }
pub trait ArtifactStore: Send + Sync { /* ... */ }
pub trait AgentAdapter: Send + Sync { /* ... */ }   // Miniforge agent supervision adapters
```

These have multiple real implementations (GitHub vs GitLab vs fake-for-tests; Claude vs Codex vs
OpenCode; S3 vs local fs; claude-code-session vs codex-session vs bash-shell). The trait is the
seam where alternative implementations actually exist.

**Domain policy is concrete.** Avoid abstracting core domain logic behind traits unless multiple
real implementations exist. A `PolicyClassifier` trait with one production impl and a test mock is
a smell — it adds dispatch overhead, indirection in every call site, and a fake in every test, for
zero benefit. Production has one classifier. The "what if we want to swap" is hypothetical and
would be cheap to add later if it ever became real.

```rust
// Good — pure function, no trait
pub fn classify_fix(violation: &PolicyViolation) -> FixClassification { /* ... */ }

// Bad — speculative trait surface with one implementation
pub trait PolicyClassifier: Send + Sync {
    fn classify_fix(&self, violation: &PolicyViolation) -> FixClassification;
}
pub struct DefaultPolicyClassifier;  // the only real impl
```

## Evidence and audit structs are append-only compatible

The Miniforge audit trail is the operator's proof of what the system did. Reinterpreting fields in
an existing version of an evidence/audit struct corrupts that proof silently — a v1 bundle written
six months ago must decode cleanly under today's code.

```rust
// Good — additive evolution
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct EvidenceBundleV1 {
    pub bundle_id: BundleId,
    pub created_at: OffsetDateTime,
    pub findings: Vec<Finding>,
    // Added in v1.1 — optional, default keeps v1.0 decodable:
    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = "Vec::is_empty")]
    pub remediation_attempts: Vec<RemediationAttempt>,
}

// Bad — same name, different shape
pub struct EvidenceBundle {              // v1
    pub bundle_id: BundleId,
    pub findings: Vec<Finding>,
}

pub struct EvidenceBundle {              // v2 — same name, repurposed shape
    pub bundle_id: BundleId,
    pub findings: Vec<EnrichedFinding>,  // different element type
}
```

When the audit shape genuinely needs to break, bump to a new versioned wire type (`EvidenceBundleV2`)
and keep the v1 decoder. See `languages/rust-wire-protocols` (234) for the general rule.

This applies to every persisted artifact: evidence bundles, policy evaluation records, agent
session transcripts, workflow snapshots, decision cards.

## Rust core does not leak UI / client assumptions

The dependency arrow points UI → core, never the reverse. CLI / web / native protocol structs can
adapt to core models; core models do not import CLI / web / TUI / SwiftUI concepts.

```rust
// Good — core model is UI-agnostic
mod domain {
    pub struct AttentionItem {
        pub id: AttentionId,
        pub severity: AttentionSeverity,
        pub summary: DiagnosticMessage,
        pub source: AttentionSource,
    }
}

// UI layers adapt at the boundary
mod tui {
    use crate::domain::AttentionItem;
    fn render_row(item: &AttentionItem, theme: &Theme) -> Line { /* ... */ }
}

// Bad — core model knows about TUI rendering
mod domain {
    pub struct AttentionItem {
        pub id: AttentionId,
        pub severity: AttentionSeverity,
        pub summary: String,
        pub tui_glyph: char,          // ← TUI concern leaked into domain
        pub tui_color: Color,         // ← so has color
    }
}
```

The same applies to web and native app surfaces: SwiftUI types, web-component descriptors, HTML
attributes, CSS classes — none of those belong in core. They get derived at the boundary by the
renderer that knows about them.

This rule is what makes the FFI / web / TUI / native shells interchangeable: they all consume the
same core model and produce their own representation. A core that imports `tui` (or `web`, or
`swiftui`) ceases to be reusable across the fleet of surface shells.

## What NOT to do

- Do not use string-typed phase / state / status fields in core logic.
- Do not collapse a policy decision into `bool` or `Result<(), String>`.
- Do not return string messages from a detector — emit a structured `Finding`.
- Do not trait-abstract a domain function that has one real implementation.
- Do not change the on-wire shape of an existing evidence/audit struct version; bump the version.
- Do not import UI / TUI / native-shell types from a core domain crate.

